.PP
/!\(rs \f[B]Do not combine these with \f[CB]|\f[B].\f[R]
.PP
On UNIX these expand to \f[CR]PROT_*\f[R] bit flags, so
\f[CR]PTR_MODE_R | PTR_MODE_X\f[R] happens to work.
On Windows they expand to \f[CR]PAGE_*\f[R] \f[B]enumerated values\f[R],
where \f[CR]PTR_MODE_R | PTR_MODE_X\f[R] produces the number
\f[CR]18\f[R], which is not a valid protection constant and makes the
call fail.
Always pass one of the combined constants directly.
.RE
.PP
Two more platform notes worth knowing:
.IP \(bu 2
Most hardware cannot express \(dqwrite but not read\(dq.
On Windows \f[CR]PTR_MODE_W\f[R] is the same value as
\f[CR]PTR_MODE_RW\f[R], and on most UNIX kernels \f[CR]PROT_WRITE\f[R]
implies read as well.
Treat \f[CR]PTR_MODE_W\f[R] as \(dqat least writable\(dq, never as a way
to hide data from a reader.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]PTR_MODE_X\f[R] alone is likewise unavailable on many targets and
is usually widened to \f[CR]PTR_MODE_RX\f[R] by the kernel.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS ALLOC
.IP
.EX
PTR ALLOC(LEN);
.EE
.PP
Allocates a raw block of memory of size \f[CR]LEN\f[R] bytes.
The returned memory is uninitialized, meaning it may contain garbage
data.
.PP
This is a direct wrapper over \f[CR]malloc\f[R] when available.
.PP
Use it when you intend to immediately overwrite the allocated memory.
.PP
For not conflicting functions like \f[CR]calloc\f[R], \f[CR]realloc\f[R]
and \f[CR]free\f[R], there is no lowercase version of these functions.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
char    *test_memory = (char *)ALLOC(42);

test_memory[0] = \(aqt\(aq
test_memory[1] = \(aqe\(aq
test_memory[2] = \(aqs\(aq
test_memory[3] = \(aqt\(aq
test_memory[4] = 0

\&...
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS CALLOC
.IP
.EX
PTR CALLOC(LEN);
.EE
.PP
Allocates a block of memory of size \f[CR]LEN\f[R] bytes and initializes
all bytes to zero.
.PP
This is a direct wrapper over \f[CR]calloc\f[R] when available.
.PP
Use it when you need predictable zero\-initialized memory (e.g.
arrays, structs, buffers).
.RS
.PP
/!\(rs Unlike the standard \f[CR]calloc(count, size)\f[R],
\f[CR]CALLOC\f[R] takes a \f[B]single\f[R] total byte count.
There is no separate element\-count argument, so no internal
multiplication overflow check is performed.
If you are allocating an array, calculate and validate
\f[CR]count * size\f[R] yourself before calling.
.RE
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
char    *test_memory = (char *)CALLOC(42);

test_memory[0] = \(aqt\(aq
test_memory[1] = \(aqe\(aq
test_memory[2] = \(aqs\(aq
test_memory[3] = \(aqt\(aq

\&...
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS REALLOC
.IP
.EX
PTR REALLOC(PTR, LEN);
.EE
.PP
Resizes a previously allocated memory block (\f[CR]PTR\f[R]) to
\f[CR]LEN\f[R] bytes.
.IP \(bu 2
If the new size is larger, the existing data is preserved and the extra
memory is uninitialized.
.IP \(bu 2
If smaller, the block is truncated.
.IP \(bu 2
May move the memory to a new location \- always use the returned
pointer.
.IP \(bu 2
If \f[CR]PTR\f[R] is \f[CR]NULL\f[R], behaves like \f[CR]malloc\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
If \f[CR]LEN\f[R] is \f[CR]0\f[R], behaves like \f[CR]free\f[R].
.PP
Use it when you need to dynamically grow or shrink buffers.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
char    *buffer = (char *)CALLOC(4);

buffer[0] = \(aqt\(aq;
buffer[1] = \(aqe\(aq;
buffer[2] = \(aqs\(aq;
buffer[3] = \(aqt\(aq;

\f[I]// resize to hold more data\f[R]
buffer = (char *)REALLOC(buffer, 8);

buffer[4] = \(aq!\(aq;
buffer[5] = \(aq!\(aq;
buffer[6] = \(aq!\(aq;
buffer[7] = \(aq\(rs0\(aq;

\f[I]// buffer now contains \(dqtest!!!\(dq\f[R]
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS FREE
.IP
.EX
void    FREE(PTR);
.EE
.PP
Deallocates a previously allocated memory block.
.PP
The pointer must either come from \f[CR]ALLOC\f[R] or \f[CR]CALLOC\f[R].
After calling \f[CR]FREE\f[R], the pointer becomes invalid and must not
be dereferenced.
.PP
This is a direct wrapper over \f[CR]free\f[R] when available.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
char    *test_memory = (char *)ALLOC(42);

\&...

FREE(test_memory);
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS LENGTH
.IP
.EX
LET LENGTH(PTR);
.EE
.PP
Returns the usable size, in bytes, of a block previously returned by
\f[CR]ALLOC\f[R], \f[CR]CALLOC\f[R] or \f[CR]REALLOC\f[R].
.PP
Every allocation carries a small hidden header immediately before the
address you receive, and \f[CR]LENGTH\f[R] simply reads the size field
back out of it.
This means the lookup is O(1) \- no scanning, no bookkeeping table.
.IP \(bu 2
Returns \f[CR]0\f[R] if the pointer is \f[CR]NULL\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
The value may be \f[B]larger\f[R] than what you originally requested,
because requests are rounded up to the allocator\(aqs alignment
granularity.
.IP \(bu 2
Passing a pointer that did not come from this allocator (a stack
address, a string literal, a pointer from the system \f[CR]malloc\f[R])
reads whatever bytes happen to sit before it.
The result is meaningless and the program may crash.
.PP
Use it when you want to carry a buffer around without carrying a
separate size variable next to it.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
char    *buffer = (char *)ALLOC(42);

\&...

\f[I]/* NO NEED TO REMEMBER \(dq42\(dq ANYMORE */\f[R]
LET size = LENGTH(buffer);

\f[B]while\f[R] (size\-\-)
    buffer[size] = 0;

FREE(buffer);
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS FAR_ALLOC
.IP
.EX
FAR_PTR FAR_ALLOC(LEN);
.EE
.PP
Allocates \f[CR]LEN\f[R] bytes of memory reachable through a far
pointer.
.PP
On 16\-bit targets this reaches memory outside the current data segment,
allowing allocations larger than 64 KB and beyond the near heap.
On 32\-bit and 64\-bit targets there is no segmentation, so
\f[CR]FAR_PTR\f[R] collapses to a normal pointer and this function
simply forwards to \f[CR]ALLOC\f[R].
.PP
Use it only when you are writing code that must also build for a
segmented 16\-bit memory model.
On every modern target it is a no\-cost alias.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
FAR_PTR huge_buffer = FAR_ALLOC(70000); \f[I]/* > 64 KB */\f[R]

\f[B]if\f[R] (!huge_buffer)
    \f[B]return\f[R] (1);

\&...

FAR_FREE(huge_buffer);
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS FAR_CALLOC
.IP
.EX
FAR_PTR FAR_CALLOC(LEN);
.EE
.PP
Same as \f[CR]FAR_ALLOC\f[R], except every byte of the returned block is
set to zero.
.PP
Like \f[CR]CALLOC\f[R], this takes a single total byte count rather than
an element count and an element size.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
FAR_PTR table = FAR_CALLOC(70000);

\f[I]/* ALL 70000 BYTES ARE GUARANTEED TO BE 0 */\f[R]

\&...

FAR_FREE(table);
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS FAR_REALLOC
.IP
.EX
FAR_PTR FAR_REALLOC(FAR_PTR, LEN);
.EE
.PP
Resizes a block previously returned by \f[CR]FAR_ALLOC\f[R] or
\f[CR]FAR_CALLOC\f[R] to \f[CR]LEN\f[R] bytes.
.PP
Behaves exactly like \f[CR]REALLOC\f[R], including the \f[CR]NULL\f[R]
and zero\-length special cases, but operates on far pointers.
The block may move, so always use the returned value and discard the old
pointer.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
FAR_PTR buffer = FAR_ALLOC(70000);

\&...

buffer = FAR_REALLOC(buffer, 140000);

\f[B]if\f[R] (!buffer)
    \f[B]return\f[R] (1); \f[I]/* OLD BLOCK IS STILL ALIVE ON FAILURE */\f[R]
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS FAR_FREE
.IP
.EX
void    FAR_FREE(FAR_PTR);
.EE
.PP
Releases a block previously returned by \f[CR]FAR_ALLOC\f[R],
\f[CR]FAR_CALLOC\f[R] or \f[CR]FAR_REALLOC\f[R].
.PP
Do not mix allocator families: a pointer from \f[CR]FAR_ALLOC\f[R] must
be released with \f[CR]FAR_FREE\f[R], and a pointer from
\f[CR]ALLOC\f[R] must be released with \f[CR]FREE\f[R].
On 16\-bit builds these use genuinely different underlying allocators,
and crossing them corrupts the heap.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
FAR_PTR buffer = FAR_ALLOC(70000);

\&...

FAR_FREE(buffer);
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS FAR_LENGTH
.IP
.EX
LET FAR_LENGTH(FAR_PTR);
.EE
.PP
Returns the usable size, in bytes, of a far block.
.PP
This is the far\-pointer counterpart of \f[CR]LENGTH\f[R] and follows
exactly the same rules: \f[CR]0\f[R] for a \f[CR]NULL\f[R] pointer,
possibly rounded up from your original request, and undefined for
pointers this module did not hand out.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
FAR_PTR buffer = FAR_ALLOC(70000);

\&...

\f[B]if\f[R] (FAR_LENGTH(buffer) < needed)
    buffer = FAR_REALLOC(buffer, needed);
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS MEMORY_MODE
.IP
.EX
BOOLEAN MEMORY_MODE(PTR, LET, BIT32);
.EE
.PP
Changes the protection of an already\-mapped region at runtime.
Returns a non\-zero value on success and \f[CR]0\f[R] on failure.
.PP
Arguments are the starting address, the length in bytes, and one of the
\f[CR]PTR_MODE\f[R] constants.
.RS
.PP
/!\(rs \f[B]The address must be page\-aligned.\f[R]
.PP
Protection is a property of whole pages, not of individual bytes.
On UNIX, passing an address that is not a multiple of
\f[CR]PAGE_SIZE\f[R] makes the underlying \f[CR]mprotect\f[R] fail
outright.
On Windows the call quietly succeeds but rounds outward, changing the
protection of \f[I]every\f[R] byte on the pages you touched \- including
data belonging to unrelated allocations that happen to share the page.
.PP
In practice this means \f[CR]MEMORY_MODE\f[R] is safe on memory from
\f[CR]ALLOC_PAGE\f[R], and unsafe on memory from \f[CR]ALLOC\f[R] /
\f[CR]CALLOC\f[R].
.RE
.PP
Also note that revoking write access does not scrub the contents; the
bytes are still there, they are just no longer writable.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
PTR region = ALLOC_PAGE(4096, PTR_MODE_RW, 0);

\&...

\f[I]/* FILL IT FIRST */\f[R]
memset(region, 0, 4096);

\f[I]/* THEN FREEZE IT */\f[R]
\f[B]if\f[R] (!MEMORY_MODE(region, 4096, PTR_MODE_R))
    \f[B]return\f[R] (1);

\f[I]/* region[0] = 1; <\-\- WOULD NOW CRASH */\f[R]

FREE_PAGE(region);
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS FAR_MEMORY_MODE
.IP
.EX
BOOLEAN FAR_MEMORY_MODE(FAR_PTR, LET, BIT32);
.EE
.PP
Far\-pointer counterpart of \f[CR]MEMORY_MODE\f[R].
Identical semantics, identical page\-alignment requirement.
.PP
On 32\-bit and 64\-bit targets it forwards directly to
\f[CR]MEMORY_MODE\f[R].
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
FAR_PTR region = (FAR_PTR)ALLOC_PAGE(4096, PTR_MODE_RW, 0);

\&...

FAR_MEMORY_MODE(region, 4096, PTR_MODE_RX);
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS ALLOC_EXE
.IP
.EX
PTR ALLOC_EXE(const PTR OP_CODES, LET);
.EE
.PP
Allocates a block of \f[B]executable\f[R] memory, copies \f[CR]LEN\f[R]
bytes of machine code from \f[CR]OP_CODES\f[R] into it, flushes the
instruction cache for that range, and returns a pointer you can cast to
a function pointer and call.
.PP
If \f[CR]OP_CODES\f[R] is \f[CR]NULL\f[R] the region is allocated but
left uninitialized.
.PP
Allocations are served from internally managed slabs, so many small
stubs share one mapping instead of burning a page each.
Freed segments are coalesced with their neighbours and reused, and a
slab is returned to the operating system once every segment inside it is
free.
.PP
This is the primitive you build a JIT, a trampoline, a thunk, or a
runtime patch out of.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]/* x86\-64: mov eax, 42 ; ret */\f[R]
static const unsigned char  CODE[] = {
    0XB8, 0X2A, 0X00, 0X00, 0X00,
    0XC3
};

LET (*answer)(void);

answer = (LET (*)(void))ALLOC_EXE((PTR)CODE, \f[B]sizeof\f[R](CODE));

\f[B]if\f[R] (!answer)
    \f[B]return\f[R] (1);

\&...

\f[I]/* PRINTS 42 */\f[R]
printf(\(dq%lu\(rsn\(dq, answer());

FREE_EXE((PTR)answer);
.EE
.SS Memory protection and what you may assume about the pointer
Executable memory is the one allocation the operating system genuinely
argues with you about, so \f[CR]ALLOC_EXE\f[R] tries strategies in order
of \f[B]speed\f[R] and takes the first the host permits.
.PP
.TS
