l l l l.
T{
Tier
T}@T{
Strategy
T}@T{
\f[CR]RW\f[R] vs \f[CR]RX\f[R]
T}@T{
Cost
T}
_
T{
1
T}@T{
One \f[CR]PTR_MODE_RWX\f[R] region.
The returned pointer is readable, writable and executable.
T}@T{
Same address
T}@T{
One syscall per slab, nothing afterwards
T}
T{
2
T}@T{
Two views of one anonymous object (\f[CR]memfd_create\f[R], an unlinked
temp file, or a pagefile\-backed section on Windows), mapped
\f[CR]PTR_MODE_RW\f[R] and \f[CR]PTR_MODE_RX\f[R].
The allocator writes through the private \f[CR]RW\f[R] view and returns
the \f[CR]RX\f[R] view.
T}@T{
Different addresses
T}@T{
More setup per slab, then free
T}
.TE
.PP
Tier 1 is what virtually every host takes.
Tier 2 only appears where the kernel refuses executable anonymous memory
outright \- OpenBSD\(aqs W\(haX enforcement, SELinux with
\f[CR]deny_execmem\f[R], or Windows with Arbitrary Code Guard enabled.
.PP
A third strategy \- mapping \f[CR]PTR_MODE_RW\f[R], filling it, then
flipping the whole slab to \f[CR]PTR_MODE_RX\f[R] and back \- was
implemented and then removed on measurement.
It costs two \f[CR]mprotect\f[R] calls per allocation \f[I]and\f[R] two
per free, plus a TLB shootdown each time, which benchmarked around
\f[B]6600 ns\f[R] per allocate/free pair against \f[B]10 ns\f[R] for
both tiers above.
It also almost never helps: hosts that refuse \f[CR]PROT_EXEC\f[R] on
anonymous memory generally refuse \f[CR]mprotect(PROT_EXEC)\f[R] for the
same reason.
.PP
macOS is a special case.
Apple does not permit tier 2 under the Hardened Runtime, so tier 1 falls
back to \f[CR]MAP_JIT\f[R] plus \f[CR]pthread_jit_write_protect_np\f[R],
which toggles the write permission of JIT pages \f[B]per thread\f[R] in
hardware.
Two consequences follow: the toggle is thread\-local, so a slab written
on one thread is not writable from another; and it covers every
\f[CR]MAP_JIT\f[R] region on that thread at once, so you cannot hold one
JIT region writable while another executes.
.RS
.PP
/!\(rs \f[B]Do not assume the returned pointer is writable.\f[R]
.PP
On tier 1 it happens to be, because the whole region is
\f[CR]PTR_MODE_RWX\f[R].
On tier 2 and under \f[CR]MAP_JIT\f[R] it is not, and writing through it
faults.
Since the tier is chosen by the host and not by you, portable code must
hand all machine code over in the \f[CR]OP_CODES\f[R] argument at
allocation time.
If you need to modify a stub, allocate a new one and free the old one.
.RE
.PP
Regardless of tier, the instruction cache is flushed over the new code
before the pointer is returned.
This matters enormously on ARM, MIPS and POWER, where the data and
instruction caches are not coherent and freshly written bytes are
otherwise invisible to the fetch unit.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS LENGTH_EXE
.IP
.EX
LET LENGTH_EXE(PTR);
.EE
.PP
Returns the size, in bytes, of an executable segment previously returned
by \f[CR]ALLOC_EXE\f[R].
.PP
Executable segments carry their own header, separate from the one used
by \f[CR]ALLOC\f[R], so \f[B]\f[CB]LENGTH\f[B] and \f[CB]LENGTH_EXE\f[B]
are not interchangeable\f[R].
Calling \f[CR]LENGTH\f[R] on an \f[CR]ALLOC_EXE\f[R] pointer reads the
wrong header and returns garbage.
.PP
Returns \f[CR]0\f[R] for a \f[CR]NULL\f[R] pointer.
As with \f[CR]LENGTH\f[R], the value may exceed what you requested
because of alignment rounding.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
PTR stub = ALLOC_EXE((PTR)CODE, \f[B]sizeof\f[R](CODE));

\&...

printf(\(dqSTUB OCCUPIES %lu BYTES\(rsn\(dq, LENGTH_EXE(stub));

FREE_EXE(stub);
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS FREE_EXE
.IP
.EX
void    FREE_EXE(PTR);
.EE
.PP
Releases an executable segment obtained from \f[CR]ALLOC_EXE\f[R].
.PP
The pointer is located by searching the slab list, so passing an address
that was never returned by \f[CR]ALLOC_EXE\f[R] is detected and ignored
rather than corrupting anything.
Double frees are likewise detected and ignored.
.PP
The segment is merged with any adjacent free segments.
When the whole slab becomes free, every mapping backing it is unmapped
and the memory goes back to the operating system.
.RS
.PP
/!\(rs Make absolutely sure no thread is still executing inside the
stub, and that no function pointer still refers to it.
Unlike a freed data block, a freed code block is unmapped rather than
recycled, so a stale call jumps into an unmapped page and the process
dies immediately.
.RE
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
PTR stub = ALLOC_EXE((PTR)CODE, \f[B]sizeof\f[R](CODE));

\&...

FREE_EXE(stub);
stub = (PTR)0; \f[I]/* DON\(aqT LEAVE A DANGLING CODE POINTER AROUND */\f[R]
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS FAR_ALLOC_EXE
.IP
.EX
FAR_PTR FAR_ALLOC_EXE(const PTR OP_CODES, LET);
.EE
.PP
Far\-pointer counterpart of \f[CR]ALLOC_EXE\f[R].
Same slab allocator, same tier ladder, same instruction\-cache flush;
only the returned pointer type differs.
.PP
On 32\-bit and 64\-bit targets it forwards directly to
\f[CR]ALLOC_EXE\f[R].
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
FAR_PTR stub = FAR_ALLOC_EXE((PTR)CODE, \f[B]sizeof\f[R](CODE));

\&...

FAR_FREE_EXE(stub);
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS FAR_LENGTH_EXE
.IP
.EX
LET FAR_LENGTH_EXE(FAR_PTR);
.EE
.PP
Far\-pointer counterpart of \f[CR]LENGTH_EXE\f[R].
Returns the size of a far executable segment, or \f[CR]0\f[R] for a
\f[CR]NULL\f[R] pointer.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
FAR_PTR stub = FAR_ALLOC_EXE((PTR)CODE, \f[B]sizeof\f[R](CODE));

\&...

printf(\(dq%lu\(rsn\(dq, FAR_LENGTH_EXE(stub));
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS FAR_FREE_EXE
.IP
.EX
void    FAR_FREE_EXE(FAR_PTR);
.EE
.PP
Far\-pointer counterpart of \f[CR]FREE_EXE\f[R].
Releases a far executable segment and carries the same warning about
dangling code pointers.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
FAR_PTR stub = FAR_ALLOC_EXE((PTR)CODE, \f[B]sizeof\f[R](CODE));

\&...

FAR_FREE_EXE(stub);
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS PAGE_SIZE
.IP
.EX
\f[B]extern\f[R] LET  PAGE_SIZE;
.EE
.PP
The size of a single memory page on the host, in bytes.
.PP
The value is cached once by the module\(aqs constructor at startup, so
reading it costs nothing \- no syscall, no branch.
Commonly \f[CR]4096\f[R], but do not hardcode that: it is
\f[CR]16384\f[R] on Apple Silicon, and \f[CR]65536\f[R] on some other
configurations.
.RS
.PP
/!\(rs \f[CR]PAGE_SIZE\f[R] is only valid \f[B]after\f[R] the module
constructor has run.
If you call into this module from your own constructor, or from anything
that runs before \f[CR]main\f[R], use \f[CR]GET_PAGE_SIZE\f[R] instead,
which computes the value on demand.
.PP
Note also that \f[CR]PAGE_SIZE\f[R] is a name several system headers
like to define as a macro of their own.
Include \f[CR]MEMORY.H\f[R] after your system headers if you hit a
redefinition.
.RE
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]/* ROUND A REQUEST UP TO A WHOLE NUMBER OF PAGES */\f[R]
LET rounded = ((wanted + PAGE_SIZE \- 1) / PAGE_SIZE) * PAGE_SIZE;
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS GET_PAGE_SIZE
.IP
.EX
LET GET_PAGE_SIZE(void);
.EE
.PP
Queries the host for its page size and returns it.
.PP
Returns the cached \f[CR]PAGE_SIZE\f[R] when it is already populated,
and otherwise asks the operating system directly \-
\f[CR]sysconf(_SC_PAGESIZE)\f[R] on UNIX, \f[CR]GetSystemInfo\f[R] on
Windows.
If the query fails, a conservative \f[CR]4096\f[R] is returned so
callers always get a usable number.
.PP
Prefer \f[CR]PAGE_SIZE\f[R] in ordinary code and reach for this only
when you cannot rely on the constructor having run yet.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
LET page = GET_PAGE_SIZE();

printf(\(dqPAGE SIZE: %lu\(rsn\(dq, page);
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS ALLOC_PAGE
.IP
.EX
PTR ALLOC_PAGE(LET, BIT32, BIT32);
.EE
.PP
Allocates whole pages directly from the operating system, bypassing the
block allocator entirely.
.PP
Arguments are the requested size in bytes, one of the
\f[CR]PTR_MODE\f[R] protection constants, and a field of extra platform
flags passed straight through to the underlying \f[CR]mmap\f[R] or
\f[CR]VirtualAlloc\f[R] (pass \f[CR]0\f[R] if you have none).
.PP
The request is rounded up to a whole number of pages, and the returned
address is page\-aligned.
This is what makes it the right input for \f[CR]MEMORY_MODE\f[R].
.PP
Use this when you need protection control, a guard page, a large arena,
or memory that is genuinely returned to the OS on release rather than
kept on a free list.
For ordinary allocations, \f[CR]ALLOC\f[R] is far cheaper.
.PP
Returns \f[CR]NULL\f[R] on failure.
Release with \f[CR]FREE_PAGE\f[R] only \- never with \f[CR]FREE\f[R].
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]/* A READ\-ONLY LOOKUP TABLE NOBODY CAN CORRUPT LATER */\f[R]
PTR table = ALLOC_PAGE(8192, PTR_MODE_RW, 0);

\f[B]if\f[R] (!table)
    \f[B]return\f[R] (1);

\&...

\f[I]/* FILL IT, THEN SEAL IT */\f[R]
MEMORY_MODE(table, 8192, PTR_MODE_R);

\&...

FREE_PAGE(table);
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS FREE_PAGE
.IP
.EX
void    FREE_PAGE(PTR);
.EE
.PP
Releases pages obtained from \f[CR]ALLOC_PAGE\f[R], returning them to
the operating system immediately.
.PP
The original length is recovered from a header stored alongside the
mapping, so you do not have to remember it.
.PP
Passing \f[CR]NULL\f[R] is safe and does nothing.
Passing anything that did not come from \f[CR]ALLOC_PAGE\f[R] is
undefined.
.RS
.PP
/!\(rs Unlike \f[CR]FREE\f[R], this genuinely unmaps the address range.
Any later read, write or execute through that pointer faults immediately
rather than silently touching recycled memory.
.RE
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
PTR region = ALLOC_PAGE(4096, PTR_MODE_RW, 0);

\&...

FREE_PAGE(region);
region = (PTR)0;
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS IS_HEAP
.IP
.EX
BOOLEAN IS_HEAP(PTR);
.EE
.PP
Returns whether \f[CR]PTR\f[R] points to a valid heap allocation.
.PP
This function can be used to verify that a pointer originates from the
heap before passing it to functions that require heap\-allocated memory.
.PP
Returns \f[CR]TRUE\f[R] if the pointer belongs to the heap, otherwise
\f[CR]FALSE\f[R].
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
char *memory = (char *)ALLOC(64);

\f[B]if\f[R] (IS_HEAP(memory))
{
    FREE(memory);
}
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS IS_HEAP_FAR
.IP
.EX
BOOLEAN IS_HEAP_FAR(FAR_PTR);
.EE
.PP
Returns whether \f[CR]FAR_PTR\f[R] points to a valid heap allocation.
.PP
This is the far\-pointer equivalent of \f[CR]IS_HEAP()\f[R].
.PP
Returns \f[CR]TRUE\f[R] if the pointer belongs to the heap, otherwise
\f[CR]FALSE\f[R].
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
FAR_PTR memory = ...;

\f[B]if\f[R] (IS_HEAP_FAR(memory))
{
    ...
}
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS IS_HEAP_EXE
.IP
.EX
BOOLEAN IS_HEAP_EXE(PTR);
.EE
.PP
Returns whether \f[CR]PTR\f[R] points to heap memory allocated by the
currently running executable.
.PP
Unlike \f[CR]IS_HEAP()\f[R], this function rejects heap pointers
originating from other loaded executables or modules.
.PP
Returns \f[CR]TRUE\f[R] if the pointer belongs to the current
executable\(aqs heap, otherwise \f[CR]FALSE\f[R].
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
char *memory = (char *)ALLOC(64);

\f[B]if\f[R] (IS_HEAP_EXE(memory))
{
    FREE(memory);
}
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS IS_HEAP_FAR_EXE
.IP
.EX
BOOLEAN IS_HEAP_FAR_EXE(FAR_PTR);
.EE
.PP
Returns whether \f[CR]FAR_PTR\f[R] points to heap memory allocated by
the currently running executable.
.PP
This is the far\-pointer equivalent of \f[CR]IS_HEAP_EXE()\f[R].
.PP
Returns \f[CR]TRUE\f[R] if the pointer belongs to the current
executable\(aqs heap, otherwise \f[CR]FALSE\f[R].
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
FAR_PTR memory = ...;

\f[B]if\f[R] (IS_HEAP_FAR_EXE(memory))
{
    ...
}
.EE
